      "body": "The conjecture relies on the concept of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) to bridge the gap between corroboration and preference, but Popper's own formal definition of verisimilitude was proven logically incoherent by Tichý and Miller in 1974. They demonstrated that for any false theory, adding true consequences does not necessarily make it closer to the truth than another false theory; in fact, under Popper's criteria, a false theory with more content can be shown to be further from the truth than a weaker false theory. Therefore, grounding the defense of scientific preference on verisimilitude rests on a technical foundation that Popper himself had to abandon, rendering the argument internally inconsistent within his own system.",
